{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["Rashi wrote (Rashi on Leviticus 12:2): <b>When she conceives and gives birth</b> — to include a woman who gives birth to a formless embryo that was dissolved and became seed-like, in which case the mother is impure as a woman who gave birth. The meaning of the matter is that the fetus was first formed in the shape of a human being and afterward was dissolved, for anything that lacks the shape of a human being is not a fetus, and anything not fit to receive a soul is not a fetus. But at the time of birth, even a dissolved embryo transmits impurity if we can recognize its form — for example, a sandal (flattened embryo) certainly makes her impure, and if not, it is in doubt. And this is the inclusion that this verse makes according to the words of our Sages (Niddah 27). And in its straightforward meaning, they said (ibid. 31): \"A woman when she conceives\" — a woman who emits seed first — gives birth to a male. Their intention is not that the fetus is made from the woman's seed, for the woman, although she has eggs like a man's testicles, either no seed at all is produced from them, or that seed does not coagulate and does nothing for the fetus. Rather, they said \"emits seed\" concerning the blood of the womb that gathers at the time of completed intercourse in the mother and adheres to the male's seed, for according to their view the fetus is formed from the blood of the female and the white matter of the man, and both are called \"seed.\" And thus they said (ibid.): a person has three partners — the man emits the white matter from which come tendons, bones, and the white of the eye; the woman emits the red matter from which come skin, flesh, blood, and hair, and the dark part of the eye. And the view of the physicians concerning formation is likewise. According to the opinion of the Greek philosophers, the entire body of the fetus is from the blood of the woman; the man contributes only the force known in their language as the hyle (prime matter), which gives form to the matter — for there is no difference between the egg of a hen that has been with a male and one that develops from a hen rolling in dust, except that the former will produce a chick and the latter will not sprout or grow because the elemental heat, which serves as its hyle, is withheld from it. If so, the word תזריע would be like זרועיה תצמיח (Isaiah 61:11). And thus Onkelos said (Onkelos on Leviticus 12:2): ארי תעדי." "<b>Her impurity</b> — an expression of the flow of a zav from her body. Another interpretation: an expression of malady and illness, for no woman sees blood without becoming ill, her head and limbs growing heavy upon her — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 12:2). And I do not know from where it would be derived that דותיה is an expression of the flow of a zav in the language of Scripture. But it is possible that it is an expression of malady, from what they said (Niddah 9a): her head and limbs grow heavy upon her. And likewise is the view of Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra), who says that דותיה is a noun, and its meaning is illness, for the blood that emerges is an illness in the woman. And in truth it is the cleansing of superfluities, and because her head and limbs grow heavy upon her, perhaps it may be called an illness. But the correct interpretation is an expression of malady, which is like an affliction and distress, as in עלי לבי דוי (Jeremiah 8:18), היה דוה לבנו (Lamentations 5:17), like נגע לבבו (I Kings 8:38) — and behold, the zivah-flow is an affliction in the woman even though it is natural to her. And likewise על ערש דוי (Psalms 41:4)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The quotation from Niddah 31a uses לשון קודש (the sacred tongue, i.e., biblical Hebrew) to mean both the Sages' rabbinic reading and the plain biblical meaning; I have rendered each occurrence by its contextual sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:47.388240467Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}